comments[5]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-01T10:48:40.483Z","harness:claude-code","text=Intake for GH-1647. Duplicate check: pm search 'symlink lock confinement' (pm-d70h, pm-nkx, pm-zwib, pm-fsyv, pm-lxa0, pm-gh1609 are all closed and cover TTL, stale takeover, extension-entry symlinks and include-linked containment, not the locks directory itself); pm list --status open/in_progress shows no lock-confinement item. Related: pm-gh1576 closes a different check/use window (expected item revisions)."
  "2026-11-01T10:55:55.720Z","harness:claude-code","Implementation: new src/core/lock/lock-confinement.ts. inspectConfinedLockDirectory lstat-walks every component between the tracker root and the lock's parent and refuses lock_path_unsafe (exit 4) on a symlink or non-directory BEFORE mkdir or the stale-cleanup gate can follow it; it runs again after the gate, and verifyConfinedLockCreation compares handle.stat() dev/ino with lstat(lockPath) plus the re-inspected directory identity, so a swap during acquisition refuses with lock_directory_replaced instead of returning a lease that excludes no one. On Linux the stray lock is located via /proc/self/fd/<fd> and removed only when its identity matches; other platforms report stray_lock_removed=false. A symlinked lock entry now refuses (lock_entry_symlink) instead of being waited on as a peer's lock, and runLockGc refuses to sweep through a symlinked locks dir (it would delete files outside the tracker). Design note: Node has no openat, so descriptor-relative creation is approximated by identity verification; the residual is a stray cleanup-gate directory inside the swapped-away real directory, which dead-owner recovery retires. Tests: 16 cases in tests/unit/core/lock/lock-confinement.spec.ts; tests/unit/core/lock + core/item lock specs + integration/locking all pass (166)."
  "2026-11-01T15:40:43.055Z","harness:claude-code","PR 1648 review follow-up (CodeRabbit). Ordering note: gate_evidence was recorded after close and release (history entry 12) because the defect-evidence gate first failed on the hosted run; the evidence describes the shipped fix and its guards and is recorded as a follow-up instead of rewriting the append-only history. Behavior change: the lock_directory_replaced refusal no longer removes the stray lock through /proc/self/fd plus a path unlink, because the unlink cannot be bound to the identity check and could delete a same-name replacement outside the tracker; the stray lock now stays where the swap pointed, the never-released context.stray_lock_removed field is gone, and context.required branches per reason (lock_entry_symlink names the entry to remove, lock_directory_replaced explains the leftover). docs/SDK.md now says the lock entry refuses only as a symlink, scopes the nothing-outside claim to the four check-time reasons, and lists readSettingsSection and writeSettingsSection in the host contract. The regular-file case moved out of the symlink-only gate so Windows runs it. Pre-existing mid-operation swap windows (gc sweep, post-EEXIST reclaim, post-create entry check against a hard link, lstat failures treated as absent) are filed as pm-jd2zim."
  "2026-11-01T17:05:15.535Z","harness:claude-code","PR 1648 second review round (CodeRabbit): inspectConfinedLockDirectory now rejects only an exact parent segment (.. or ../...) or an absolute relative path, so a confined first segment that merely starts with two dots (for example ..cache) is no longer refused as lock_path_outside_tracker; a spec covers both. docs/SDK.md lists lock_directory_replaced as the fifth context.reason with context.lock_component naming the replaced directory."
  "2026-11-01T19:14:39.750Z","harness:claude-code","PR 1648 third CodeRabbit round: lock confinement compared dev/ino as JavaScript numbers, which lose precision above Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER (64-bit file ids on NTFS, ReFS and some network filesystems), so two distinct entries could compare equal during a directory swap. LockEntryIdentity now holds exact bigints from fs.lstat/fs.stat/handle.stat with { bigint: true }. A spec stubs inodes 2^53 and 2^53+1, which collide as numbers, and proves the swap still refuses with lock_directory_replaced; it fails with a numeric comparison."
